---
description: 
globs: 
alwaysApply: true
---
- Use complete code blocks.
- Prioritize solution-oriented replies with code.
- Avoid generating duplicate logic.
  - Always check whether similar logic or functionality already exists before writing new code.
- Maintain consistency in code style and structure.
- Clearly separate environments: development (dev), testing (test), and production (prod).
- Review code responsibly.
  - Only allow code merges after thorough self-review, or confirm the changes are well-understood and directly relevant to the requirements.
- Use feature flags / toggle modes for unfinished or high-risk features.
  - Optimize for forward compatibility where possible. If a migration is required, ensure the old logic is removed in sync to avoid redundancy.
- Avoid hardcoding data directly into the code.
  - Especially avoid writing one-time scripts into core files (e.g., data initialization scripts).
- Control the length of a single code block.
  - If a file or function exceeds 200–300 lines, consider refactoring.
- Use mock data **only** in development/testing environments.
  - Mock data is strictly prohibited in production environments.
- Do **not** generate `.env` or `.xc` files.
  - Changes must be confirmed and approved before proceeding.